#{[17592186392600 :answer/value "q(x)=\\begin{cases}x,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ x-2,\\, & x\\ge -2\\end{cases}"] [17592186393250 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{3e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=e,$ and the upper bound is $b=3e.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3e-e}{n}=\\frac{2e}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{3e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(e+i\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393257 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}2{x}^{-4}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=2{x}^{-4},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-(1)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}2{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+l\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{3l}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[2{(1+\\frac{3l}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393264 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-2{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=-2$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(-2+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393271 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=2$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-2}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394030 :question/problem "![](images/93/939f859ef3454e31a9adaae7d72210ef670f93cbf3d77ecf87aa3879d8d0644c.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}0, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ \\frac{x}{1+{y}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ \\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186396045 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{3(n+1)}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(x-1)^{3n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{n}^{3}(x-1)^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1{|}^{3} & <1 \\\\ |x-1| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-1 & <1 \\\\ 0<x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(0,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x-1|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(2-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis series is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n\nConsequently, the interval of convergence is $I=[0,2].$"] [17592186397488 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-5),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 2\\end{cases} \\\\ u(t-5) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<5 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 5.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu(t-2)+u(t-5)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\, & 2\\le t<5 \\\\ 2,\\, & t\\ge 5.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{5}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{2}^{5}+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-5s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-5s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-5s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s},\\,s>0.\n$$"] [17592186397558 :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}2,\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\, & t>3.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}."] [17592186397558 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}2,\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\, & t>3\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{t-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 1.$\n\nFor $s>1,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{(1-s)t}}{1-s}\\right)|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{1-s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{(1-s)a}-{e}^{3(1-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{1-s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3(1-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{3(1-s)}}{s-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>1.$"] [17592186397587 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}x+2, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186398095 :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{8}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)"] [17592186398277 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2n+2}}{{2}^{2n+2}((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}{{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}(n+1)^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that $R=\\infty .$"] [17592186398347 :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-2,4,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨2,5,-3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $B(1,5,13)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $b=⟨1,5,13⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =b+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨1,5,13⟩+s⟨-2,4,1⟩+t⟨2,5,-3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186399350 :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2(u-1)^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2(2-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{z}^{3}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is divergent."] [17592186399384 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+5)^{n+1}}{(n+1)(n+2)}\\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)}{(x+5)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{n(x+5)}{n+2}| \\\\  & =|x+5|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{n+2} \\\\  & =|x+5|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}\\right) \\\\  & =|x+5|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x+5|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+5| & <1 \\\\ -1<x+5 & <1 \\\\ -6<x & <-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-6,-4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x+5|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-6$ and $x=-4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=-6$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-6+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=-4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}},\n$$\nwe see that the series is convergent by the comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=[-6,-4].$"] [17592186399440 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=4?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}5, & x<4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10, & x\\ge 4\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}5-2x, & x\\le 4 \\\\ x+3, & x>4\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}5x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 5, & x=4\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186399447 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=7?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}2x-1, & x\\le 7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-30, & x>7,\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}, & x<7 \\\\ 5x+14, & x\\ge 7,\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{x-7}, & x<7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}, & x\\ge 7,\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186399447 :question/worked-solution "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(7)=2(7)-1=13$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}(2x-1)=2(7)-1=13\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}({x}^{2}-30)={7}^{2}-30=19.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(7)=5(7)+14=49$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}{x}^{2}=49, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}(5x+14)=5(7)+14=49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 7}f(x)=49.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,7}g(x)=49=g(7).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(7)=\\frac{1}{7}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-7}=-\\infty \n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nSince the left limit is infinite, $h(x)$ does not have a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$"] [17592186400093 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$”,  the dominant term in the denominator is $2{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}}\\right)}{(\\frac{2{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$”, the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}-n)}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-n}{0-1} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}$ for $n\\ge 1$”,  the limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$” diverges."] [17592186400100 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{({n}^{3}+1)({n}^{3}-1)}{n(2-n)}=\\frac{{n}^{6}-1}{2n-{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{6}-1}{2n-{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{6}-1}{2n-{n}^{2}} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{6}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{({n}^{4}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2}{n}-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}-0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence diverges."] [17592186400233 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x{n}^{3/2}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =3|-x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{3},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{3},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{3}\\right)}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.   So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}{\\left(\\frac{3}{3}\\right)}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.   So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}].$"] [17592186402402 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\,(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402495 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402495 :question/worked-solution "The right-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$"] [17592186402662 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-1)^{3}, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{6}+1, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186402675 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402922 :question/problem "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{3}-1 & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ 2(x-1), & x>1\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}3x+2, & x<1 \\\\ 4, & x=1 \\\\ -3x+8, & x>1\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}{e}^{x-1}, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1\\end{cases}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402922 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={1}^{3}-1=0, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =2(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $g(1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)+2=5, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & =-3(1)+8=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)=5.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 1}g(x)\\ne g(1)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $h(1)={e}^{(1-1)}=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}h(x) & ={e}^{(1-1)}=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}h(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 1}h(x)=h(1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=1.$"] [17592186402929 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}3x-1, & x\\ne -1 \\\\ 4, & x=-1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186402929 :question/worked-solution "For $x=-1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(3x-1)=3(-1)-1=-4, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(3x-1)=3(-1)-1=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-4.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\nf(-1)=4\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x).\n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nHence, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$” is true, and “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-1.$” are false."] [17592186402991 :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x)$ using the product  rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a minimum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is negative to the left of the critical point and positive to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}x & (0,\\frac{1}{e}) & \\frac{1}{e} & (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ) \\\\ \\hline f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ \\hline {f}^{′} & - & 0 & +\\end{array}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval $(\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ).$"] [17592186403265 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=9.$ \n\nApplying the root rule, we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}\\frac{\\sqrt{25-f(x)}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(25-f(x))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{25-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186405002 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)}^{3/2}|x| \\\\  & =|x|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<1.$ So, the radius of convergence is $1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-1,1).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-1$ and $x=1$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.  So the endpoint $x=1$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=-1$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-1,1].$"] [17592186405016 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 2$”,  the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$”, the limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$”, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}-3)}{(1+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{1+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 2$” diverges."] [17592186407295 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d66cb97b9e04367ce2250e5156eaa9daaf42fadb395220e0464bca687e91908.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186407295 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,4]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-4,4]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,4]$” only."] [17592186407846 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\frac{{0}^{3}-2(0)}{{2}^{3}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{y({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{y}({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [17592186407847 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186407848 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}-2}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2}-2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2})^{2}-4}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy-2}{(xy-2)(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} & =\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1+2)(\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{16}.$"] [17592186407849 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\frac{1+(-1)}{\\sqrt{2-1}-\\sqrt{2+(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z})(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(x-y)-(x+z)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{-(y+z)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(y+z)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{\\cancel{(y+z)}} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z}) & =-(\\sqrt{2-1}+\\sqrt{2+(-1)}) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [17592186413321 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x}{5{x}^{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9x+1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(5+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9x+1-0)}{(5+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(9x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186413322 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{7}+3}{{x}^{4}+6x+2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{7}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(1+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-{x}^{3}+0}{1+0+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186413323 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}+1}{{x}^{3}-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+0)}{(1-0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186413415 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}8xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{2}{3}).\n$$"] [17592186413415 :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}8xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8y{\\int }_{0}^{y}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8y{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & =4y({y}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\begin{cases}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8xy}{4{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{2}{3}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{2x}{{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{[{x}^{2}]}_{1/4}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\frac{3}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186416142 :question/worked-solution "The dominant term in the denominator is $8{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{5}-2n}{8{n}^{4}-2} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2n}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{8{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8n-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})}{(8-\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8n-0}{8-0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }n \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges."] [17592186416522 :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{2}{11}, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{11}, & x=-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{11}, & x=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$"] [17592186419943 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}1-\\frac{x}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>2\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186419943 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0\\le x\\le 2,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}(1-\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={(t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<0 \\\\ x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186419944 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{225}{(x+15)^{2}}, & x\\ge 0,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X\\le 5).$"] [17592186419945 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nfind $P(2<X\\le 4).$"] [17592186419946 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{21}, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nfind $P(3<X<4).$"] [17592186419947 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186419947 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{6}{{t}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}}|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186419948 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<0 \\\\ 1-{e}^{-6x}, & x\\ge 0,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nfind $P(5\\le X\\le 8).$"] [17592186419949 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{2}{15}(6-x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<2 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>5.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186419949 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $2\\le x\\le 5,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<2 \\\\ {\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{2}{15}(6-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{15}t(t-12)|}_{2}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}x(x-12)-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+12x-20}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<2 \\\\ \\frac{-{x}^{2}+12x-20}{15}, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186419952 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{3}{8{x}^{4}},\\, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0,\\, & x<\\frac{1}{2}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186419952 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge \\frac{1}{2},$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{x}\\frac{3}{8{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{8{t}^{3}}|}_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{x}^{3}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{8{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186419953 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186419953 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186419954 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{60}{x}^{3},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<2 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186419954 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $2\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<2 \\\\ {\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 2\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{1}{60}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{240}|}_{2}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{240}-\\frac{1}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{240}-\\frac{1}{15}, & 2\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186419957 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}0, & x<4 \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 4,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X>6).$"] [17592186419958 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{e}^{-|4x|},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x>0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186419958 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\begin{cases}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\le 0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x>0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals. \n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{2},\\, & x>0.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186421865 :question/problem "Compute $P(X<-5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}4{e}^{4x},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x>0.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186421866 :question/problem "Compute $P(X>3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186425676 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ 3, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186425677 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ {x}^{3}, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186425678 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3}+3, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ x+1, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186425680 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186427361 :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(-2{x}^{-1/2})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436123 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{1+(-1)}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+z)+y(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}x+y & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"] [17592186436124 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{3+(-3)+4}-2}=\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{4}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y+4-4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{z(x+y)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2) & =2(\\sqrt{3-3+4}+2) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}+2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $8.$"] [17592186436125 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y} & =\\frac{2(2\\cdot 1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436126 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the expression under the radical   is negative. However, for the point $(-2,-2)$ the product $x\\cdot y$ is not negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot (-2)}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436127 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{({0}^{2})(0)}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}(0)}{{x}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186436128 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2(1)^{2}(-1)+1(-1)^{2}}{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2})}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)(x+y)}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{x+y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y) & =1(1-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"] [17592186436129 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(4,-4,-2)$ does not cause the  denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(4,-4,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4-(-4)}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{8}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436130 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6+3}-3}{6+3-{3}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y}-x)(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y})^{2}-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{6+y-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$"] [17592186444116 :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2))(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{1-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-2.$ This gives\n\n{f}^{″}(-2)=-\\frac{1}{(1-(-2))^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+2)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{9}\\right)}{2}=-\\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186475943 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & =\\frac{(1-1)(2-2)}{(1-1)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0(0)}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(1,2)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (1,2)$ along the path $y=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)\\,\\,\\,y=2}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(2-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(0)}{(x-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (1,2)$ along the path $y=x+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)\\,\\,\\,y=x+1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x+1-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(x+1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x-1)}{(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)^{2}}}{2\\cancel{(x-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186476884 :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{(x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{4}{(x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}(x+y)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}(x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=1}^{y=b} \\\\  & =-2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}]}_{y=1}^{y=b} \\\\  & =-2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{(x+b)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{2}{(u+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{(u+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{[-\\frac{1}{u+1}]}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{x+1}] \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{x+1}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=\\frac{x-1}{x+1}.$"] [17592186476889 :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $y\\le 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\lim_{b\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{b}^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\lim_{b\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{b}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\lim_{b\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{b}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $y\\le 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{b}^{y}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{u}]}_{b}^{y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to -\\infty }({e}^{y}-{e}^{b}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{Y}(y)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0 \\\\ 1 & y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(y)={e}^{y}.$"] [17592186476948 :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{y=1}^{y=b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[1-\\frac{1}{b}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=\\frac{x-1}{x}.$"] [17592186478423 :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x-y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [0,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,0\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{b}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186478458 :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3b}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2y{e}^{-3x}}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(3{e}^{-3x})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186479070 :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right){|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(1-0)+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}+(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186480675 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{0+(-3)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186480684 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=4 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-3)+{f}^{+}(-3)}{2}=\\frac{4+(-2)}{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186480697 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}) \\\\  & =9y\\left(\\frac{1\\,200-y}{1\\,200}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9y}{1\\,200}(1\\,200-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{400}(1\\,200-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,200.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,200.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=1\\,200.\n$$"] [17592186481762 :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{2-1}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ are divergent."] [17592186486356 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-18{x}^{2}}{12p{x}^{2}-3x+9} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{18{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{12p{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-18)}{(12p-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-18}{12p-0+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2p} & =-2 \\\\ 4p & =3 \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486357 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{n}^{4}+3}{4n-5{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-5{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{n}^{4}+3}{4n-5{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{4n}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{5{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7+\\frac{3}{{n}^{4}})}{\\frac{4}{{n}^{3}}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+0}{0-5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-\\frac{7}{5}.$"] [17592186486359 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(4n-1)(4n+1)}{n(7-16{n}^{2})}=\\frac{16{n}^{2}-1}{7n-16{n}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{16{n}^{2}-1}{7n-16{n}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-16{n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{16{n}^{2}-1}{7n-16{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{16{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{7n}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{16{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{16}{n}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{7}{{n}^{2}}-16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0-16} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186486362 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(1+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"] [17592186486364 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-1}{3{x}^{3}+5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{-1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{-1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(3+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{3+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"] [17592186486372 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{27}{{x}^{3}-8} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{27}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{27}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{(1-0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
